{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nAgents sometimes botch the `rebar-ticket:` trailer, leaving a ticket's change unlinkable at close time. The `COMMITS` event type and `attach_commits` reducer already exist (`rebar.attach_commits`, defined at `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:697` and re-exported from `src/rebar/__init__.py`) — used by the code-review workflow. What is missing is an author-facing repair surface and a consumer. Approved design (analysis ticket b902-18af-14ed-4da1): expose retroactive commit linking on ALL THREE surfaces (CLI, Python library, MCP), document it clearly, and then add a cheap deterministic close-gate check that the linked change's diff is consistent with the recorded file_impact. Explicitly OUT of scope: using the linked commits as a completion-verifier diff basis — linked commits may be incomplete even when they match file_impact, and CV already prefetches from file_impact.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **Shared DET module** `src/rebar/_engine_support/commit_impact.py` (new, ~120 LOC) holding the two deterministic helpers this feature needs, so no logic is re-derived and nothing is inlined into the near-cap `_lib_writes.py` (778/800 LOC): (a) `invalid_commit_shas(shas, repo_root)` — which SHAs fail `git rev-parse --verify <sha>^{commit}`; (b) the path matchers for step 4, including `glob_match`, which is the repo's established `**/`-aware semantics (`fnmatch(path, pat) or (pat.startswith(\"**/\") and fnmatch(path, pat[3:]))`) currently at `src/rebar/llm/prompting/prompts.py:746` and mirrored in `llm/code_review/registry.py` and `grounding/oracle.py`. Because the DET close path must NOT import `rebar.llm` (the optional `[agents]` extra), the helper is hoisted HERE and `prompts._glob_match` becomes a thin delegating wrapper — one implementation, no fourth copy.\n\n2. **CLI verb** `rebar attach-commits <ticket> <sha>...` wrapping the existing `rebar.attach_commits` seam. Registration follows the established dispatch seam: add the verb to `_WRITES_FULL` in `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py` (661 LOC), add a handler to `_ARGV_REGISTRY` in `src/rebar/_commands/__init__.py` (variadic — it takes N SHAs), and ship pinned help as `src/rebar/_cli/help/attach-commits.txt` plus the matching line in `src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt` (golden-pinned; `tests/interfaces/contracts/test_help_overview_coverage.py` asserts every known subcommand appears there). The thin CLI body lives in `src/rebar/_commands/attach_commits.py`.\n\n3. **SHA validation in the SEAM, not the CLI**, so all three surfaces inherit it identically: `rebar.attach_commits` calls `commit_impact.invalid_commit_shas` before appending. **Validation is ALL-OR-NOTHING** — every supplied SHA is validated BEFORE any event is appended; if one or more fail to resolve, NO `COMMITS` event is recorded and the call raises an error naming every offending SHA. A unit test asserts this (a mixed valid/invalid batch leaves `state.commits` unchanged). Union-add semantics unchanged. Library surface: `rebar.attach_commits` is already importable and re-exported — confirm and document. MCP: register an `attach_commits` tool in `src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py` (384 LOC) with an `AttachCommitsResultOut` outputSchema model in `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` (390 LOC), following the existing write-tool pattern; these are the real MCP write-tool modules (`mcp_server.py` only assembles registrars).\n\n4. **DET close check**, added to the existing completion precheck in `src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py` (394 LOC) — the module that today raises the \"cannot confirm the work landed\" error. There is no `src/rebar/workflow/close_gate.py`. Commit-set discovery is exactly the two sources that already exist, unioned: (i) `state.commits` populated by COMMITS events (the attach-commits surface added here), and (ii) the referencing-commit scan the precheck already performs (`_referencing_commit_exists`, `close_precheck.py:94` — commits reachable from the worktree HEAD carrying a `rebar-ticket:` trailer or leading `<id>:` subject for the ticket or any transitive descendant). That function returns only a bool, so it is refactored into a SHA-returning `_referencing_commits(...)` with `_referencing_commit_exists` kept as a thin wrapper over it — the documented monkeypatch target and its call sites keep working unchanged. For each discovered commit, `git show --name-only` gives the changed paths; a changed path that is neither covered by the recorded `file_impact` nor matched by this feature's OWN exemption globs (`tests/**`, `docs/**`, `**/*.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` — defined and documented here, not inherited from any other gate) is a deterministic close BLOCK naming the offending commit and paths. **Matching semantics** (all via step 1's module, not re-derived): both sides are normalized repo-relative POSIX paths; a `file_impact` entry matches on exact equality, or as a directory prefix when the entry ends with `/` or names an existing directory; exemption globs use the `**/`-aware `glob_match`. No substring matching. No LLM. A ticket with no discovered commits keeps today's behavior (the existing cannot-confirm-work-landed error), and a ticket without `file_impact` is out of scope for this check.\n\n5. **Remedy for a legitimate block** (so the tightened gate has a clear escape path): the block message itself names the offending paths and tells the author how to proceed — the fix is `rebar set-file-impact <ticket> …` to amend the ticket's `file_impact` to declare the additional path, after which the close proceeds; the pre-existing `--force=\"<reason>\"` override remains the break-glass. Stated in the block message AND in the docs of step 6.\n\n6. **Documentation.** `docs/cli-reference.md` is **generated** (`scripts/gen_cli_reference.py`, with a CI drift gate and a subcommand count in its header), so it is REGENERATED rather than hand-edited. Hand-edited: `docs/mcp-reference.md` (the new tool + its outputSchema) and the commit-trailer guide `src/rebar/_guides/commit-ticket-trailer.md` (surfaced by `rebar explain commit-trailer`). Each names the repair verb, when to use it, and the step-5 remedy.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar attach-commits <ticket> <sha>` records a COMMITS event and `rebar show` reflects it; invalid SHAs are rejected with a clear error naming them, and a batch containing ANY invalid SHA records NOTHING (all-or-nothing). Verify: `env PATH=\"$PWD/.venv/bin:$PATH\" python -m pytest tests/unit/test_attach_commits.py -x` passes, including a test asserting `state.commits` is unchanged after a mixed valid/invalid batch.\n- [ ] The MCP server exposes `attach_commits` with an outputSchema and the Python library documents the call; both route through the same seam as the CLI. Verify: the MCP tool-inventory test lists `attach_commits` and passes.\n- [ ] With file_impact recorded and linked commits present, a commit touching a path outside the declared impact deterministically blocks close with the offending paths named; a conforming diff does not block; an exempt path (`tests/**`, `docs/**`, `**/*.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`) does not block, including at the repo root. Verify: unit tests cover all three directions.\n- [ ] The DET check imports no `rebar.llm` module and the `**/`-aware glob semantics have exactly one implementation. Verify: a test asserts no `rebar.llm*` module is in `sys.modules` after running the DET check in a fresh interpreter, and that `prompts._glob_match` delegates to `commit_impact.glob_match`.\n- [ ] The block message and the docs name the verb, the repair workflow, and the `set-file-impact` remedy; `docs/cli-reference.md` is regenerated and its drift gate passes. Verify: grep assertions plus `python scripts/gen_cli_reference.py --check`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786513714177774001,"uuid":"4e203254-0f17-4476-b849-f96045f99b5e"}